  "recipeCuisine": "Japan",
  "name": "Katsudon",
  "text": "Katsudon\n\nPreparation time:\n\nCooking time:\n\nYield: 1\n\nIngredients\n\nFor the Tonkatsu\n\n120g 1cm thick pork loin,\n\na pinch of salt,\n\na pinch of pepper,\n\nflour,\n\n1/4 egg,\n\na dash of water,\n\nNama-Panko (fresh bread crumbs)\n\nFrying Oil\n\nFor the sauce\n\n50ml dashi,\n\n1 tbsp soy sauce,\n\n1 tbsp mirin,\n\n1 tsp sugar,\n\nTo complete the dish\n\n1/4 onion (cut into thin slices),\n\n1 egg.\n\nTo Garnish\n\nMitsuba (Japanese wild parsley, roughly chopped)\n\nServe with\n\n200g steamed rice.\n\nPreparation\n\n1. Prepare the pork loin by making cuts across the tough parts, including the fat. Pound the loin with a meat hammer to tenderize (not to make it thinner).\n\n2. Season one side of the loin with salt and pepper.\n\n3. Put 1/4 egg into a shallow dish and beat lightly.\n\n4. Coat both sides of the pork loin with flour using a brush, dip the floured pork loin into the beaten egg and coat with breadcrumbs.\n\n5. Deep fry at 170C until golden brown,turning once.\n\n6. Drain the fat and cut into 1” pieces.\n\n7. Add the dashi stock to a medium frying pan with the soy sauce, mirin, sugar and onion.\n\n8. With the lid on, bring to the boil, then simmer for 3 minutes.\n\n9. Add the pork loin to the pan, then cover with the beaten egg.\n\n10. With the lid on cook on high heat for 30 seconds.",
  "recipeYield": "1",
